          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p2\",\"quote\":\"Often the only clues for trans-Neptunian objects (TNO) is a crude estimate of their diameters and albedos.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p6\",\"quote\":\"In 2010, Gonzalo Tancredi presented a report to the IAU evaluating a list of 46 trans-Neptunian candidates for dwarf planet status based on light-curve -amplitude analysis and a calculation that the object was more than 450 kilometres (280 mi) in diameter. Some diameters were measured, some were best-fit estimates, and others used an assumed albedo of 0.10 to calculate the diameter.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p15\",\"quote\":\"Beside the five older accepted by the IAU plus Quaoar, the 'nearly certain' category includes Gonggong , Sedna , Orcus , Máni , and Salacia . Note that although Brown's site claims to be updated daily, these largest objects have not been updated since late 2013, and no objects have been added to the list since late 2020; indeed, the current best diameter estimates for Salacia and Máni are less than 900 km. (Orcus is just above the threshold.)\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p20\",\"quote\":\"This suggests that the threshold for dwarf planethood in the trans-Neptunian region is a diameter of ≈1000 km (thus including Pluto, Eris, Haumea, Makemake, Gonggong, Quaoar and possibly Sedna; Orcus was not addressed).\"}]}"
        }
